{-
   What a `String` is on this target - Implementation-String.md part 2's growable row, and exactly
   `Array(CodeUnit)`'s two words - declared as the record it is, on `Array`'s terms.

   `String` is a primitive in the shipping stage (see Type::String), because on JS it is the host
   string and a wrapper there would cost an allocation per string. That leaves the native half
   needing somewhere to say what the bytes *are*, and this is it: `@builtin(string)` is how the
   compiler finds the record, and its one field is the same run-plus-count a container uses, so a
   string's capacity, its placement tag and its growth path are `Run`'s and not a second
   implementation of them. The name is the primitive's on purpose - a program that writes `String`
   means the type both targets agree on, and what this declares is what that type is made of here.
   The ownership stage has no primitive and this *is* its `String`; `prelude/Core/String.yana` is
   the same declaration.

   `length` is the count in native units - UTF-8 bytes here - which part 3 is careful to say is not a
   portable number. It is the run's *live prefix*; `run.capacity` is what was allocated, and the two
   differ for exactly the reason they differ in an array.

   **The bytes are an `Array(CodeUnit)`, and this is a one-field wrapper over it** rather than a
   second record declared field for field identically. A separate record means `String.reserve` and
   `reserve` are the same geometric growth written twice and `String.pushUnit` and `push` the same
   store-and-bump; this way there is one of each, and copy-on-write is a `Run(a)` property the
   string inherits rather than a rule anyone writes.

   `CodeUnit` and not `U8`, which is what makes `instance Chunked(String, CodeUnit)` in
   `Core/Text.native.yana` the true statement it reads as: a string *is* a container of this
   target's own encoding units, and saying so is what puts it on the vector path. The two are the
   same eight bits here and the type says which of them is meant - see TargetInt in the compiler,
   where `CodeUnit` stopped being a name for a width.

   **Which record a field of a string is on is the position rule** - Analysis-Spellings.md item 13,
   and Analysis-Manifestations.md Part 8's view table. `&self: String`, an owned string and a `->`
   one are the **owner**, so `self.units` there is this array: growable, its run reachable. `self:
   String` in a binding position is the **view**, `Core/String.yana`'s `StringView`, so `self.units`
   there is the address of the units and `self.length` their count, and nothing that could grow the
   string is reachable through it. That is the statement `[a]` makes about an array with a bracket,
   made by the position alone because a string has no bracket spelling; the compiler reads the
   view's fields through `Viewed.view` beside that record, and reads none of them itself.

   **The wrapper is not ceremony.** `&Array(CodeUnit)` is a *slice* - `resolveType`'s Borrow case
   says so, and that is the fixed point of the container design: a borrow of a container is a
   descriptor, never an address of the owner. The string algorithms need the owner, because growing
   is what they do, and `&self: String` is how they ask for it.

   The record itself is **Core's**, in `Core/String.yana` beside the view and the `Viewed` instance
   that derives one - a string is a Core type, and every Core file that names one would otherwise
   have to import this module to do it. What stays here is what actually needs saying: the raw
   address below and `stringLiteral` further down, which forges a string out of bytes nothing has
   validated. That is the act this module's import is a warning about, and it is still behind it.
-}

{-
   The code unit address a string is defined by - `values` for a string, and for the same reason:
   the result is rooted in the string, so a read through it is a read of the string.

   `self.units` here is the *view's* field, since `self` is a shared string: two words, of which this
   is the first. Here rather than in Core because what it hands out is a raw pointer, and handing
   one out is this module's business - `stringBytes` in `CString.native.yana` is the same address
   as bytes.
-}
@inline
pub fn units(self: source'String) -> %source'CodeUnit = self.units

{-
   A string, from bytes that already exist - Implementation-String.md part 9.

   The bytes are a constant in the module's data and this is the two words that describe them.

   **A written literal no longer comes through here.** `resolveString` emits the whole value as a
   constant global and duplicates it bitwise, which is the same two words with the call removed and,
   more to the point, with the placement flag readable as a constant by whatever tests it - see
   `LiteralFold.yana`, and `inheritConstant` in opt_place.cpp. What still comes through here is a
   literal whose *bytes* are chosen at run time: a constructor map's string table holds an address
   and a length per constructor and builds the value from the pair it read (see resolve/map.h).

   **`runBorrowed` is what makes a literal free, and it is also what makes it grow correctly.** The
   run does not own its slots, so `releaseRun` hands nothing back and a literal costs no teardown at
   all. And `resize` refuses only a `runFixed` run - a *borrowed* one it relocates, by allocating,
   copying the old capacity and freeing nothing, since there was nothing of its own to free. That is
   copy-on-write, and it falls out of Implementation-Containers.md §2's existing three answers rather
   than needing a fourth: appending to a literal copies it to the heap once, and appending again does
   not.

   `capacity` is the byte length rather than zero, because the bytes really are there to be read -
   this is what lets a literal be indexed and compared without the run ever being touched.

   **Deliberately not `@inline`, and the measurement is why.** Copying this body into every caller
   was once the only way to put `ownsHeap` in front of the teardown that tests it - see
   Analysis-Borrows.md §8.9 - and over `test/resolve` it was **+445 lines of lowered IR and three
   more `Reclaim(String)` calls, for nothing**: the construction inlined, the *teardown* did not, so
   the program paid for both. The constant global has since made that route unnecessary for a
   written literal, and the measurement stands for the callers that remain.
-}
pub fn stringLiteral(bytes: %CodeUnit, length: Int) -> String =
    String {units: Array {
        run: Run {items: bytes, capacity: truncate(length) :: Count, ownsHeap: runBorrowed},
        length: truncate(length) :: Count
    }}
